section:: Timing and examples

FFT analyzers read a matching FFT chain without changing its representation. Declare the same FFT size and hop in stateful analyzers. Audio-input analyzers accumulate their own frames or envelope windows. Results normally hold between analysis frames; onset triggers last one control block. Memory is allocated in constructors. Consult each class for startup and smoothing delay.

Boot the server, run an example's parenthesized setup block, and run its cleanup block before starting another example. Defaults read ExampleFiles.child and include a commented SoundIn alternative. Audible examples are demonstrations of behavior, not proofs of accuracy on every recording.

code::
(
Routine({
    ~mir2Buffer = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.child);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1);
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);
        var pitch = Pitch2.kr(source, 60, 1500);
        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(2048), source, 0.25);
        var onset = Onsets2.kr(chain, fftSize: 2048, hop: 0.25);
        var tone = SinOsc.ar(pitch[0].clip(40, 1500).lag(0.03), 0, pitch[2].lag(0.02) * 0.05);
        var click = Decay2.ar(K2A.ar(onset[0]), 0.001, 0.035) * PinkNoise.ar(0.08);
        (source * 0.15 + tone + click) * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.3) ! 2
    }.play;
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
~mir2Buffer.free;
)
::

section:: Implementation status and references

This remains an unpublished development expansion. The full library roadmap is tracked in docs/roadmap-status.md; tonal/harmonic, rhythm, wavelet and constant-Q work must not be inferred from this guide until their public classes are listed. Neural methods and PNCC have separate reference and licensing gates. See ORIGINS.md and the family implementation reports for primary sources and evaluation limits. MIR2 complements FluCoMa's corpus, decomposition, and data workflows.
